From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 13:45:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] i386: long long division by a constant without the libgcc
 helpers

gen_opl() no longer calls __divdi3, __moddi3, __udivdi3 or __umoddi3 for
a division or modulo by a constant: a power of two is a shift or a mask
(with gcc's bias for a signed dividend), and on i386 any other divisor
of at most 32 bits is two divl (the new pseudo-op TOK_UDIVL of gen_opi():
edx:eax / r32 with the quotient in the low and the remainder in the high
word; q_hi = hi / d, then (hi % d : lo) / d), a signed one through the
unsigned division of |x| with the sign restored.  Freestanding code such
as the Linux kernel has no libgcc, and gcc strength-reduces every
division by a constant, so a gcc-built 32-bit kernel never links them.

Three more things the kernel's i386 objects needed for that, all of what
gcc does for them: __builtin_mul_overflow() of 64-bit operands checks the
product through its 32-bit halves instead of dividing (check_mul_overflow
in kstrtox.c, fs/buffer.c, sched/fair.c, ptp_clock.c); a static function
whose body is one return statement is inlined when given a constant
argument (ieee80211_tu_to_usec(SLOT_TU) is the divisor of a u64 in
mac80211_hwsim_nan.c); an expression statement without an effect is
dropped (dead_expr_stmt(): block/badblocks.c's `rounddown(s, 1 <<
bb->shift);` statements, whose 64-bit modulo by a non-constant has no
other way out), probed without code the way inline_fold_check() probes
a stub body, its tokens saved and replayed.

tccgen_finish() pops the macro stack before the saved bodies are freed:
an error inside a body left the body string on the stack for
preprocess_end() to read after it was freed.
---
